{"tool_name":"neptune","contents":["biocontainers","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"neptune","identifiers":["biotools:neptune","doi:10.1093/NAR/GKX702"],"license":"Apache-2.0","summary":"Neptune: genomic signature discovery","home_url":"https://github.com/phac-nml/neptune","keywords":["Bacillus anthracis","Bacteria","Computational Biology","DNA Mutational Analysis","Gene Expression Regulation, Bacterial","Genetic Association Studies","Genome, Bacterial","Microbiological Techniques","Sequence Analysis, DNA","Transcriptome","Vibrio cholerae","Genetic variation","Genomics","Machine learning","Genotype and phenotype"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":0},"biotools":{"id":"neptune","home":"https://phac-nml.github.io/neptune/","summary":"Locate genomic signatures using an exact k-mer matching strategy while accommodating k-mer mismatches. The software identifies sequences that are sufficiently represented within inclusion targets and sufficiently absent from exclusion targets. The signature discovery process is accomplished using probabilistic models instead of heuristic strategies.","addition_date":"2018-05-21T18:35:09Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-25T14:25:30.884354Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:neptune","credit":[{"email":"eric.marinier@phac-aspc.gc.ca","name":"Eric Marinier","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"name":"Natalie Knox","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6133-7799","typeEntity":"Person"},{"name":"Rahat Zaheer","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3307-4666","typeEntity":"Person"}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://phac-nml.github.io/neptune/install/"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Nucleic acid sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2478"}]}],"name":"Neptune","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"migle_sur","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkx702","metadata":{"abstract":"The ready availability of vast amounts of genomic sequence data has created the need to rethink comparative genomics algorithms using 'big data' approaches. Neptune is an efficient system for rapidly locating differentially abundant genomic content in bacterial populations using an exact k-mer matching strategy, while accommodating k-mer mismatches. Neptune's loci discovery process identifies sequences that are sufficiently common to a group of target sequences and sufficiently absent from nontargets using probabilistic models. Neptune uses parallel computing to efficiently identify and extract these loci from draft genome assemblies without requiring multiple sequence alignments or other computationally expensive comparative sequence analyses. Tests on simulated and real datasets showed that Neptune rapidly identifies regions that are both sensitive and specific. We demonstrate that this system can identify trait-specific loci from different bacterial lineages. Neptune is broadly applicable for comparative bacterial analyses, yet will particularly benefit pathogenomic applications, owing to efficient and sensitive discovery of differentially abundant genomic loci. The software is available for download at: http://github.com/phac-nml/neptune.","authors":[{"name":"Bekal S."},{"name":"Berry C."},{"name":"Chen J."},{"name":"Chui L."},{"name":"Domaratzki M."},{"name":"Farber J."},{"name":"Graham M.R."},{"name":"Isaac-Renton J."},{"name":"Knox N.C."},{"name":"Mabon P."},{"name":"Mahony J."},{"name":"Marinier E."},{"name":"Pagotto F."},{"name":"Patterson-Fortin L."},{"name":"Prystajecky N."},{"name":"Reimer A.R."},{"name":"Seyer K."},{"name":"Slade P."},{"name":"Tremblay C."},{"name":"Van Domselaar G."},{"name":"Weedmark K.A."},{"name":"Zaheer R."},{"name":"Zhang J."}],"citationCount":16,"date":"2017-10-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"Neptune: A bioinformatics tool for rapid discovery of genomic variation in bacterial populations"},"pmcid":"P